#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079423} :activity/id #uuid "ff014b49-75da-5266-b4b5-9e7bd3e5b3d8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048186 :topic/id #uuid "869fb66c-d668-58cf-9dd4-b10651dbe9d5" :topic/math-academy-id 634} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079423 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079675 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the graph of ![](images/c9/c956e6cbfb2d94fa56a68a3173ec78f60fee33a9066c9c44ca4096d67d7b1cba.svg) shown below.\n\n![](images/2c/2cb61ee785ff6e04c5b73281389d6653f42bc93636da6840fbc2391ffb82451c.png)\n\nHow can we use graph transformations to plot the graph of\n\n![](images/c5/c546f9b96ef07d40050b7d98f046528f54ea43afc5d42c1bac48d2a02406e079.svg)\n\nSince this function involves several transformations, we apply them in the following order:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of ![](images/a0/a047a10a3ed3f5e86676e8fca21093abfe6a1849d90ce94b62101f34fca37e3d.svg) We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of ![](images/a0/a047a10a3ed3f5e86676e8fca21093abfe6a1849d90ce94b62101f34fca37e3d.svg) We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nIn this case, we begin by factoring out the coefficient next to the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) inside the function: \n\n![](images/e9/e905676d5234ac091212cf51bce10510cdde7e6c5000aabb564dfee38ca4c020.svg)\n\nNow, to plot ![](images/18/1867234b91fecd21c650bf6b29134a695d20150a98990c6c7b65ffed0a858a8a.svg) we apply the following steps:\n\n- Consider the curve ![](images/e2/e2ed4a9881134f510823ba710f835acf5720b866f0df86fa6cd24448524431d6.svg)\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of ![](images/9a/9ae11798da63cafee6ce5ccf824a7b96f9f7407a4ee445d1431d907c36f5dd1b.svg) parallel to the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis to get the graph of ![](images/a1/a142ed820c2bdafb8c1b7969028c7f4533c66fa772a73954c46f6b5eb91a050f.svg)\n\n![](images/07/07fe84bddbdc721dfba2fc9b720ca79ff794e9846fa1ef3bbc0c3c2217edd4e8.png)\n\n- Then, translate it to the right by ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) units to get the graph of ![](images/d0/d027e035caec097035ee8f20e49d28dadb156445265efe674460854cce152406.svg)\n\n![](images/a1/a16ccd8e56c64f80f93476f2b7a41aa3093a2e4b9fdbe089fa502d1f1df15923.png)\n\n- There is no vertical scaling, so we omit this step.\n\n- Finally, translate it up by ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) unit to get the graph of ![](images/f6/f6d6c8ff90e58f882bbd48d3b52f37f8c1657a5a45218d31c481c2a400e25ac3.svg)\n\n![](images/f4/f4a894860fadbc3ac88fa360cae23ef0f33903f3d930cb00496bb2c2505d2334.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bf539881-a530-50df-b39d-b9718839371f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4616 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e6e20122-6866-5fbc-9527-7a17b8c290e8" :step/math-academy-id 28409 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079425}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079424 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078510 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078360 :question/id #uuid "bbd2751e-73b2-500b-abef-c67564a9559f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7622"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c2387a4-202d-528a-b0ca-3f87d5a06ea3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549039}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Correct Order of Transformations Given a Transformed Function Expression"} :step/id #uuid "a78dc881-a3e2-5e40-bd1b-cf28fe897545" :step/math-academy-id 28411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078511 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078361 :question/id #uuid "c7dbe419-31b2-5c5a-bcab-738aa6fd46fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-7623"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17339421-a1ad-554f-aed8-28e20cebab10" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050085}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526039}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Transformations Resulting From Three Operations"} :step/id #uuid "a6368e4f-8808-5bd9-b6a8-0a2f253f3384" :step/math-academy-id 28412 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079426}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079426 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078512 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078362 :question/id #uuid "6ee9bee5-8027-5ba5-ad2c-9b697f2affc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7624"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d69a3ef2-0854-503b-8586-b0113966d9ad" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526043}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Transformations Resulting From Four Operations"} :step/id #uuid "6856acd9-d60c-536e-80e4-da84599cde69" :step/math-academy-id 28413 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079424}}] :activity/title "Combining Graph Transformations: Three or More Operations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078658}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549124} :activity/id #uuid "41da6bc7-ca27-5127-aecd-da9516e3970d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048186 :topic/id #uuid "869fb66c-d668-58cf-9dd4-b10651dbe9d5" :topic/math-academy-id 634} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16989c60-1053-533b-9f14-b9ea3d24df0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548396} {:answer/id #uuid "3df56a22-be89-53fc-a186-949e5208a495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548472} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f27536-7f52-5f15-81d2-99bca9257fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548743} {:answer/id #uuid "e587188b-268f-535d-aee2-c34d70d9f812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548803} {:answer/id #uuid "fa5d3062-3532-5d26-b4cb-17046684e70f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548857}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548396} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab07e844-c0d5-5c67-88f0-da904b950fef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0e1845bd-ab9c-5876-9015-5e45dd5e00ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-87488" :question/problem "The graph of $y=4f(2x)-3,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Translate up by $3$ units\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Translate down by $3$ units\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $0.5$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=4f(2x)-3,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4).$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}=0.5$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2).$ Stretch $y=f(2x)$ by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=4f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3).$ Translate $y=4f(2x)$ down by $3$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=4f(2x)-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}3).$"} :step/id #uuid "10c35e87-699a-5a06-ba8b-912ea39e334d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db0e1b6-b15e-5702-b4eb-23afbb278ad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548385} {:answer/id #uuid "3ead96e5-9ccc-55be-bf55-9fff19987ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548474} {:answer/id #uuid "84c6764a-4bfc-516a-86e1-dea5f1cc141f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}5)\\to (\\text{T}3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548611} {:answer/id #uuid "9130b0d1-296f-54fe-866a-5d9499a8522f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548639} {:answer/id #uuid "b047744c-b2e2-51d7-b12c-1fa93bce8cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548702}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548385} :answer-field/id #uuid "014d1aa7-6876-598e-b36b-73f23d2e7df3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "56fcd68f-be15-5bdd-9b31-95eea95afa89" :question/math-academy-id "q-87046" :question/problem "The graph of $y=7f(2x-2)+5,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Translate up by $5$ units\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $7$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Translate right by $1$ unit\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $0.5$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}5)$ - Translate right by $2$ units\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=7f(2x-2)+5=7f(2(x-1))+5\n$$\n\nTo get $y=7f(2(x-1))+5,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4).$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}=0.5$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3).$ Translate $y=f(2x)$ right by $1$ unit to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2(x-1)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2).$ Stretch $y=f(2(x-1))$ by a scale factor of $7$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=7f(2(x-1)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}1).$ Translate $y=7f(2(x-1))$ up by $5$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=7f(2(x-1))+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}1).$"} :step/id #uuid "8ee2a0af-2eaa-5542-8abb-4d17722ea629" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549039 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b94df81-13c9-5d2e-8eb9-88bce32c5d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548402} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6a4c4d-e439-579f-b03d-3f49d6e8866e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548528} {:answer/id #uuid "5f43e829-377d-588f-b3f4-35d2ad572442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548530} {:answer/id #uuid "a56f0fc4-5c0f-5083-b821-55e535ad2c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548681} {:answer/id #uuid "c075a111-f868-5188-9ba9-3a5f5292a779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\text{T}1)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548736}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548528} :answer-field/id #uuid "1db9c8b6-c498-58eb-a73c-ab6c2a805e78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9454265-8028-5150-af82-6933624c324f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112955" :question/problem "The graph of $y=3f(5x-15),$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $0.2$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Translate right by $3$ units\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=3f(5x-15)=3f(5(x-3))\n$$\n\nTo get $y=3f(5(x-3)),$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2).$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{5}=0.2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(5x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4).$ Translate $y=f(5x)$ right by $3$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(5(x-3)).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}1).$ Stretch $y=f(5(x-3))$ by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=3f(5(x-3)).\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}1).$"} :step/id #uuid "f9c0e085-a181-5a3a-a5b2-0ef40761da79"}] :activity/title "Combining Graph Transformations: Three or More Operations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548340}]}
